// The apex, laterite.dev — static assets on a Worker with no code. Third of
// three: `laterite-app` (app.), `laterite-docs` (docs.), this one (bare).
//
// It exists because the apex was ANSWERING NOTHING while being advertised: the
// GitHub Pages redirect shim names laterite.dev in its canonical tag and its
// noscript fallback, so every crawler and every JS-less visitor following that
// shim was sent to a hostname with no record. A redirect rule would have closed
// that too, but a Worker is the thing the real landing page is — so this is the
// same object, thinner, rather than scaffolding to tear down.
//
// `assets.directory` is ./dist — the output of the landing page's OWN Vite
// build (landing/vite.config.ts, `npm run build:landing`), not a hand-written
// file, since #394. It is gitignored, so a deploy that skips the build step
// ships nothing rather than something stale.
//
// No `not_found_handling`: the default is an honest 404. The app's
// single-page-application setting would answer every mistyped path with the
// landing page, which is wrong for a site that is one real page.
{
  "$schema": "../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "laterite-site",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-14",
  // Production stays OFF workers.dev — the apex custom domain below is the
  // only front door — while preview URLs stay ON, so `wrangler versions
  // upload` can put a branch build on an unguessable
  // <version-prefix>-laterite-site.<subdomain>.workers.dev for review
  // without shifting a byte of production traffic. Both stated rather than
  // defaulted: `preview_urls` otherwise inherits `workers_dev`, so turning
  // the front door off would silently take the review lane with it.
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": true,
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
  },
  "routes": [{ "pattern": "laterite.dev", "custom_domain": true }],
}
